WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.273
Diffusion models produce stunning samples, but they
are slow,

00:00:04.353 --> 00:00:09.327
often needing a thousand sequential denoising steps
to generate a single sample.

00:00:09.407 --> 00:00:13.875
This paper from Stanford asks a different question
than most prior work.

00:00:13.955 --> 00:00:18.297
Instead of cutting the number of steps and paying
with sample quality,

00:00:18.377 --> 00:00:21.329
can we keep every step but run them in parallel,

00:00:21.409 --> 00:00:23.855
trading extra compute for lower latency?

00:00:23.935 --> 00:00:26.382
The answer is a method called ParaDiGMS,

00:00:26.462 --> 00:00:33.583
which uses Picard iterations to guess the whole
denoising trajectory and refine it in parallel until it converges.

00:00:33.663 --> 00:00:38.700
It delivers two-to-four times faster sampling with
no measurable drop in quality.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:43.111
The central limitation of diffusion models is
sampling speed.

00:00:43.191 --> 00:00:49.042
A standard denoising diffusion probabilistic model
can take a thousand sequential passes

00:00:49.122 --> 00:00:52.277
through the neural network to create one sample,

00:00:52.357 --> 00:00:55.108
which is far too slow for interactive use.

00:00:55.188 --> 00:01:00.567
The popular fix is to simply use fewer denoising
steps, as DDIM and DPMSolver do,

00:01:00.647 --> 00:01:04.274
but reducing steps comes at the cost of sample quality.

00:01:04.354 --> 00:01:08.520
The field has largely accepted this
quality-for-speed tradeoff.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.629
The authors pursue an orthogonal direction.

00:01:11.709 --> 00:01:14.046
Instead of trading quality for speed,

00:01:14.126 --> 00:01:17.182
they ask whether we can trade compute for speed.

00:01:17.262 --> 00:01:21.297
The goal is to lower the latency of generating a
single sample,

00:01:21.377 --> 00:01:24.106
not just the throughput of generating many.

00:01:24.186 --> 00:01:26.131
At first this seems impossible,

00:01:26.211 --> 00:01:31.488
because denoising is inherently sequential: each
step depends on the previous one.

00:01:31.568 --> 00:01:35.407
Naive parallelization can generate multiple samples
at once,

00:01:35.487 --> 00:01:40.960
but making a single sample appear faster in
wall-clock time is a much harder problem.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.621
The key contribution is ParaDiGMS,

00:01:43.701 --> 00:01:47.441
short for Parallel Diffusion Generative Model Sampling.

00:01:47.521 --> 00:01:53.343
It is the first general method that lets you spend
extra parallel compute to sample a

00:01:53.423 --> 00:01:55.704
pretrained diffusion model faster,

00:01:55.784 --> 00:01:57.302
without any retraining.

00:01:57.382 --> 00:02:00.843
Crucially, it is orthogonal to existing techniques,

00:02:00.923 --> 00:02:06.746
so it can be layered on top of DDIM or DPMSolver to
yield ParaDDIM and ParaDPMSolver,

00:02:06.826 --> 00:02:08.899
combining both axes of speedup.

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:12.510
It is also compatible with classifier-free guidance.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:15.314
The method builds on Picard iterations,

00:02:15.394 --> 00:02:21.030
a classic technique for solving ordinary
differential equations by fixed-point iteration.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:28.607
The insight is to write the value at each timestep
as the initial value plus the integral of the drift along the path.

00:02:28.687 --> 00:02:31.497
Starting from a full guess of the trajectory,

00:02:31.577 --> 00:02:36.313
ParaDiGMS updates every timestep simultaneously
using the cumulative drift,

00:02:36.393 --> 00:02:39.074
and repeats until the values stop changing.

00:02:39.154 --> 00:02:43.441
Because each iteration can be computed in parallel
across timesteps,

00:02:43.521 --> 00:02:48.707
and the number of iterations to converge is much
smaller than the number of steps,

00:02:48.787 --> 00:02:51.404
the whole trajectory resolves much faster.

00:02:51.484 --> 00:02:56.220
In practice it uses a sliding window of a fixed
size to respect GPU memory,

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:00.330
advancing the window as soon as the earliest
timesteps converge.

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:05.536
The experiments cover two very different domains to
show generality.

00:03:05.616 --> 00:03:11.668
On the robotics side, the authors test diffusion
policies on Robosuite Square, PushT,

00:03:11.748 --> 00:03:12.966
and FrankaKitchen,

00:03:13.046 --> 00:03:17.872
measuring task reward averaged over hundreds of
evaluation episodes.

00:03:17.952 --> 00:03:19.171
On the image side,

00:03:19.251 --> 00:03:25.519
they evaluate StableDiffusion version 2 generating
768-by-768 images from COCO captions,

00:03:25.599 --> 00:03:30.497
judged by CLIP score, and pixel-space LSUN Church
and Bedroom models,

00:03:30.577 --> 00:03:31.940
judged by FID score.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:38.000
Together these span latent-space and pixel-space
diffusion at very different scales.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:45.883
The headline finding is remarkably consistent: a
two-to-four times speedup across every task and every sampler,

00:03:45.963 --> 00:03:48.752
with no measurable degradation in quality.

00:03:48.832 --> 00:03:50.050
On robotic control,

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:56.267
ParaDDPM speeds up sampling by roughly 3.4 to 3.9
times while holding task reward constant.

00:03:56.347 --> 00:03:58.248
On StableDiffusion version 2,

00:03:58.328 --> 00:04:03.782
it brings the time to generate an image down from
fifty seconds to under fifteen,

00:04:03.862 --> 00:04:08.359
a 3.4x gain, and stacking it on faster samplers
reaches four times.

00:04:08.439 --> 00:04:14.234
The number of parallel iterations needed for
convergence is up to twenty times smaller

00:04:14.314 --> 00:04:16.694
than the number of sequential steps,

00:04:16.774 --> 00:04:18.880
which is why the approach works.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:25.157
The main ablation studies the effect of the error
tolerance in the fixed-point iteration.

00:04:25.237 --> 00:04:30.529
A lower tolerance means more iterations and slower
sampling but higher fidelity,

00:04:30.609 --> 00:04:32.879
while a looser tolerance is faster.

00:04:32.959 --> 00:04:40.266
The paper shows there is a comfortable regime where
a fairly relaxed tolerance still preserves sample quality.

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:42.146
Importantly, on LSUN Church,

00:04:42.226 --> 00:04:46.847
ParaDDPM matches full DDPM's FID score at nearly
four times the speed,

00:04:46.927 --> 00:04:51.682
whereas simply reducing DDIM to 500 steps produces
visibly worse images,

00:04:51.762 --> 00:04:57.390
demonstrating that the gains genuinely come from
parallelism rather than fewer steps.

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:03.290
To put concrete numbers on it: sampling speedups of
two to four times across the board.

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:09.148
StableDiffusion version 2 drops from fifty seconds
to fourteen-point-six seconds per image.

00:05:09.228 --> 00:05:14.233
On LSUN Church, FID barely moves, from
twelve-point-eight to twelve-point-nine,

00:05:14.313 --> 00:05:16.743
while running almost four times faster.

00:05:16.823 --> 00:05:22.279
And the deep reason it scales is that the number of
parallel iterations to converge is

00:05:22.359 --> 00:05:26.270
up to twenty times smaller than the thousand
sequential steps.

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:31.117
The lasting takeaway is a new axis for accelerating
diffusion models.

00:05:31.197 --> 00:05:34.675
Rather than sacrificing quality by taking fewer steps,

00:05:34.755 --> 00:05:38.760
ParaDiGMS spends parallel compute to run all the
steps faster,

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:43.373
cutting sampling latency by two to four times with
no loss in quality,

00:05:43.453 --> 00:05:47.195
and it composes with the fast samplers people already use.

00:05:47.275 --> 00:05:50.753
Looking forward, as parallel hardware keeps improving,

00:05:50.833 --> 00:05:55.761
sampling time will be limited only by the small
number of Picard iterations,

00:05:55.841 --> 00:05:58.990
pointing toward even faster real-time generation.
